  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A ridge of high pressure will build\neastward across Northern Nevada this afternoon and Thursday. A\nwarming trend is expected this afternoon and Thursday. High\ntemperatures throughout Northern and Central Nevada this afternoon\nwill be around five degrees warmer than yesterday afternoon.\nMaximum temperatures across Northern and Central Nevada Thursday\nafternoon will be about ten degrees above seasonal values. \n\nAn anticyclone aloft will be centered over Southern Nevada Friday.\nThis upper-level area of high pressure will move eastward across \nthe Desert Southwest this weekend. High temperatures each\nafternoon Friday through Sunday will be around fifteen degrees\nabove normal for this time of year. Near record warmth is\nanticipated in Elko, Ely, and Tonopah Friday afternoon. Near\nrecord warmth is expected in Elko, Winnemucca, and Ely Saturday\nafternoon. Near record warmth is anticipated in Elko and Ely\nSunday afternoon. Here are probabilities of maximum temperatures \nof 85 degrees or higher for select locations in Northern and \nCentral Nevada on Saturday, October 3rd, 2026: \n\nBattle Mountain - 97%\nCarlin - 97%\nElko - 96%\nWinnemucca - 96%\nEureka - 80%\nWells - 80%\nTonopah - 75%\nSpring Creek - 60%\nEly - 55%\nWest Wendover - 45%\n\nA trough of low pressure will pass eastward across the Silver\nState Monday. This will result in a downward temperature trend and\npossibly a light shower or two. Be that as it may, high \ntemperatures will remain well above seasonal values both Monday \nafternoon and Tuesday afternoon.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence\nregarding a warming trend this afternoon and Thursday. High\nforecast confidence in unseasonably warm weather Friday through\nSunday. Moderate forecast confidence regarding near record warmth\nin most of Northern and Central Nevada both Friday afternoon and\nSaturday afternoon. Moderate forecast confidence in near record\nwarmth in parts of Northern and Central Nevada Sunday afternoon.\nNo deviations from the NBM model output.",
